{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792571140504,"sessionID":"ses_ea26f370bffeBZoOhIu3UU3u5d","part":{"partID":"prt_15d90e8c9001aiiwaJckBdvTr5","sessionID":"ses_ea26f370bffeBZoOhIu3UU3u5d","messageID":"msg_15d90d5e4001Qvj61slNi8sy4W","type":"tool","id":"call_01_ET_iHGTv7eu5jKjbDoUbHMF0210","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-auxiliary-pilot-2026-10-21/home-care/calls/discovery/leaf.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-auxiliary-pilot-2026-10-21/home-care/calls/discovery/leaf.md, lines 1-258\n1: # Home Maintenance Application\n2: \n3: ## Overview\n4: \n5: A **Home Maintenance Application** helps a homeowner stay on top of the recurring upkeep of their own home: it holds the home's care targets, turns them into a schedule of recurring care work, and keeps a record of the care that was done.\n6: \n7: The defining structure is small:\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: The user's own home (structure, systems, equipment) as the subject of care\n11: └── Recurring upkeep plan (care items with cadences → due work over time)\n12:     └── Upkeep record (completions accumulating into the home's care history)\n13: ```\n14: \n15: Everything else commonly associated with these products — reminder notifications, built-in task libraries, appliance databases with manuals and warranties, receipts, pro recommendations, rewards — is widespread in current products but is not part of the defining core. The paper home-maintenance schedule pinned in a house book satisfies the same structure without any of them.\n16: \n17: When the center of gravity shifts — to the household's routine labor (chores), to the whole-home information binder, to possession records, or to planned change-work — the product is drifting toward a different Application Type.\n18: \n19: ## Users & Context\n20: \n21: The primary user is a homeowner responsible for a residential property — a first-time buyer facing an unfamiliar list of systems to care for, or a long-time owner keeping up a known routine.\n22: \n23: Typical reasons to open the application:\n24: \n25: - see what care is due now or coming up this season\n26: - check how to do a care task, or who did it last time and what it cost\n27: - record that a task was completed, with photos, receipts, or cost\n28: - look up an appliance's manual, warranty, or service history\n29: \n30: Secondary users are household members who share the care work. The context is consumer and personal: one's own home, used occasionally but over many years, mostly from a phone. Small-scale landlords who track upkeep across multiple rental properties are at the edge of this Type — at scale their needs belong to professional property-maintenance systems.\n31: \n32: ## Core Model\n33: \n34: ### The Defining Core\n35: \n36: ```text\n37: The user's own home (structure, systems, equipment) as the subject of care\n38: └── Recurring upkeep plan (care items with cadences → due work over time)\n39:     └── Upkeep record (completions accumulating into the home's care history)\n40: ```\n41: \n42: Three properties. If any one is removed, the product is no longer recognizable as a home maintenance application:\n43: \n44: - **The home as the anchored subject of care.** The application holds a persistent record of the user's own home, and its care targets are the home's physical fabric — structure, systems, and equipment (roof, gutters, HVAC, water heater, detectors, appliances). Tasks hang from this record. Without it, the product is a generic to-do or reminder app, or a content site with no home of record.\n45: - **The recurring upkeep plan.** Care items are held as scheduled, repeating work — seasonal, annual, monthly, or usage-based cadences that generate due work over time. Without the forward-looking plan, the product is a one-off repair log or home journal.\n46: - **The upkeep record.** Completions are tracked and retained — what was done, when, and often at what cost and by whom — accumulating into the home's care history. Without it, the product is a static checklist or a reminder-only tool that never learns what has actually been done.\n47: \n48: The three are load-bearing together: a plan without a record is a tips checklist; a record and schedule without the home anchor is a generic recurring to-do list; a home record with history but no plan is a service log.\n49: \n50: ### Standard Capabilities\n51: \n52: Mature products commonly add these. They make the upkeep practical but do not define the Type:\n53: \n54: - **Reminders and notifications** — due-date prompts delivered through the product's channels (text and email are observed; mobile apps also prompt in-app), so scheduled care actually happens.\n55: - **Home profile** — the home's characteristics (location, age, systems, appliances) used to tailor which care items apply and when.\n56: - **Appliance and equipment records** — per-device entries with make/model, manuals, and warranties, anchoring care tasks to specific equipment.\n57: - **Service history with receipts** — logged completions carrying cost, provider, and documents.\n58: - **Guidance content** — short how-to instructions attached to care tasks.\n59: - **Household sharing** — more than one person can see and act on the plan.\n60: - **Calendar or seasonal views** — the plan rendered as what's due this month or this season.\n61: \n62: ### One Structure, Many Implementations\n63: \n64: The Core Model is written in conceptual terms. Products realize each concept differently:\n65: \n66: ```text\n67: Concept:            The home as subject of care\n68: Implementations:    a home profile with systems and appliances; a binder pre-loaded\n69:                     from an inspection report; a property record in a household hub\n70: \n71: Concept:            The recurring upkeep plan\n72: Implementations:    built-in task libraries filtered by home characteristics;\n73:                     user-defined recurring tasks; prompts generated from the profile\n74: \n75: Concept:            The upkeep record\n76: Implementations:    check-off with timestamps; a log of actions with receipts and\n77:                     costs; service records attached to appliances\n78: ```\n79: \n80: A reader who has only seen one implementation should still be able to recognize the others from the Core Model.\n81: \n82: ## How It Works\n83: \n84: ### Set up the home and its care targets\n85: \n86: ```text\n87: Add the home (location, type, age)\n88: → add its systems and equipment (in partner-distributed products the home\n89:    may arrive pre-loaded from an inspection report)\n90: → the application proposes applicable care items\n91: → adjust the plan: add, remove, re-cadence\n92: ```\n93: \n94: The home record is the anchor: every task, appliance, and receipt belongs to this home.\n95: \n96: ### Work the plan over time\n97: \n98: ```text\n99: Care items come due on their cadences\n100: → reminders surface what's due (and what's overdue)\n101: → the homeowner does the work (DIY, guided by attached instructions)\n102:    or hires it out (some products recommend or connect to pros)\n103: → completion is recorded: date, notes, photos, cost, receipt, provider\n104: → the cycle resets; the next due date is computed\n105: ```\n106: \n107: This is the interaction loop of the Type: due work → done work → recorded work → next due work. Over years the record becomes the home's care history — useful for warranties, resale, and deciding when replacement beats repair.\n108: \n109: ### Look up and decide\n110: \n111: ```text\n112: Open an appliance or system record\n113: → see its manual, warranty, and past service\n114: → decide the next action (maintain, repair, replace)\n115: ```\n116: \n117: ### Core vs Common vs Optional\n118: \n119: **Defining core** — without these, not a home maintenance application:\n120: \n121: - the user's own home as the anchored subject of care\n122: - recurring upkeep plan with cadences\n123: - upkeep record / care history\n124: \n125: **Common mature structure** — present in most current products:\n126: \n127: - reminders and notifications\n128: - home profile tailoring the plan\n129: - appliance/equipment records with manuals and warranties\n130: - service history with receipts and costs\n131: - guidance content, household sharing, calendar/seasonal views\n132: \n133: **Optional / variant** — depends on product and segment:\n134: \n135: - pro recommendations or booking (marketplace leg)\n136: - rewards for completed care\n137: - recall alerts from stored make/model numbers\n138: - sustainability/carbon tracking\n139: - multi-property support\n140: - self-hosted/open-source deployment\n141: - distribution through real-estate professionals (pre-loaded binders)\n142: \n143: ## Interfaces\n144: \n145: The following surfaces are described in conceptual terms. Exact layouts and names vary by product.\n146: \n147: ### Home overview\n148: \n149: The entry surface for the home as a whole.\n150: \n151: - the home's profile (location, type, age), its systems and appliances at a glance\n152: - what's due now, overdue, and coming up\n153: - primary actions: open the plan, add an appliance, record a completion\n154: \n155: ### Upkeep plan / task list\n156: \n157: The schedule surface.\n158: \n159: - care items grouped by cadence, season, or system; due and overdue states\n160: - primary actions: mark done, snooze/reschedule, edit cadence, add a custom task\n161: \n162: ### Task detail\n163: \n164: The surface for one care item.\n165: \n166: - cadence, last done, next due, instructions, attached photos/receipts\n167: - primary actions: complete with notes/cost/receipt, view history, edit\n168: \n169: ### Appliance / equipment record\n170: \n171: The per-device surface.\n172: \n173: - make/model, manual, warranty, purchase details, past service entries\n174: - primary actions: log service, attach document, link care tasks\n175: \n176: ### Care history / log\n177: \n178: The record surface.\n179: \n180: - chronological completions with dates, costs, providers, documents\n181: - primary actions: filter by system or year, export or share (in some products)\n182: \n183: ### Settings / sharing\n184: \n185: Household members, notification preferences, and (where offered) pro connections.\n186: \n187: ## Important Rules / Behaviors\n188: \n189: ### Cadence drives the plan\n190: \n191: A care item's recurrence (seasonal, annual, monthly, usage-based) is what generates due work. Completing a task computes its next due date; the plan is self-perpetuating. Exact cadence options vary by product.\n192: \n193: ### The record is per-home and durable\n194: \n195: Everything — tasks, appliances, completions, receipts — belongs to the home record and survives years of use. This is what makes the history valuable at resale or for warranty claims.\n196: \n197: ### The plan is tailored, not universal\n198: \n199: Care items apply conditionally: a home's location, climate, age, and equipment determine which tasks apply and when. The same product proposes different plans for different homes.\n200: \n201: ### Reminders are prompts, not enforcement\n202: \n203: The application surfaces due work; completing it remains the homeowner's responsibility. The plan does not disappear when ignored — items stay due until done or changed.\n204: \n205: ### DIY and hired work both feed the record\n206: \n207: Whether the homeowner does the work or a pro does, the completion is recorded against the home. Some products recommend pros; the record remains the homeowner's.\n208: \n209: ## Variants\n210: \n211: Common realizations of the Type:\n212: \n213: - **suite-embedded** — maintenance reminders and histories as one capability of a whole-home binder (documents, inventory, projects, pros) distributed through real-estate professionals\n214: - **log-and-rewards** — a home-anchored care log with prompts, receipts, and incentives for completed care\n215: - **appliance-anchored** — the plan built outward from per-device records, manuals, and warranties\n216: - **household-ERP / self-hosted** — upkeep machinery (equipment, recurring tasks, batteries) inside an open-source household system; documents the boundary toward chore/household tools\n217: - **pro-connected** — the plan joined to recommended or bookable service professionals\n218: - **multi-property** — the same upkeep machinery across several homes; at scale this drifts toward professional property-maintenance systems\n219: \n220: A variant remains a **Variant** while the defining core holds. When the center of gravity shifts to the household's routine labor, the whole-home binder, possession records, or planned change-work, the product belongs to the neighboring Type.\n221: \n222: ## Related Application Types\n223: \n224: | Application Type | Distinction |\n225: |---|---|\n226: | Household Chore Application | recurring tasks too, but the subject is the household's routine living work (cleaning, laundry), not the home's physical fabric |\n227: | Home Management Application | the whole-home binder/hub is the center; maintenance machinery is one capability beside documents, inventory, and finance |\n228: | Home Inventory Application | the record is of possessions; appliance records overlap, but recurring-work machinery is the maintenance app's center |\n229: | Home Improvement Planner | bounded, discretionary change-work (renovation, remodel) with a money plan, vs recurring preservation of existing systems; products bundle both with separate machinery |\n230: | Property Maintenance Management | the professional/landlord operations system (work orders, vendors, tenants, portfolios); different subject of record and user |\n231: | Home Services Marketplace | the hiring leg (finding and booking pros) vs the homeowner's upkeep plan and record; complementary |\n232: | Home warranty / service-plan products | a service contract (coverage, claims, dispatch) vs the homeowner's own care plan; warranty apps may carry reminders as engagement features |\n233: | CMMS / EAM | the same abstract shape (assets + recurring preventive tasks + history) but for organizations' physical assets; different subject, user, and scale |\n234: | Smart Home Platform | device control and automation vs upkeep planning |\n235: \n236: The most important boundary is with the Household Chore Application, because both hold recurring tasks with completion tracking. The structural difference is the task's subject: the home's fabric versus the household's routine.\n237: \n238: ## Representative Products\n239: \n240: - HomeBinder — suite-embedded pole; binder distributed through home inspectors, lenders, and agents, with maintenance reminders and recall alerts\n241: - Dwellin — log-and-rewards pole; home profile, appliance records, maintenance logging, prompts, rewards\n242: - Under My Roof — Apple-ecosystem suite module; maintenance beside inventory, renovations, documents, and insurance\n243: - Grocy — household-ERP / self-hosted pole; equipment, chores, and batteries machinery documenting the chore boundary\n244: \n245: The dedicated maintenance-plan suite pole (HomeZada class) could not be reached during research and is intentionally not characterized here.\n246: \n247: ## Sources\n248: \n249: Research date: **2026-09-08**\n250: \n251: - HomeBinder — https://www.homebinder.com/\n252: - Dwellin — https://dwellin.com/ , https://dwellin.com/app/how-it-works/ , https://dwellin.com/app/overview/\n253: - Grocy — https://grocy.info/\n254: - Under My Roof — via the paired Home Inventory research pass of 2026-09-08 (official surfaces reachable then; direct fetch timed out this pass)\n255: \n256: > Sourcing limitation: several prominent products in this category were unreachable from the research environment on 2026-09-08 (HomeZada, BrightNest, HomeSmarts, HomeKeepr returned blocks or empty responses; Centriq and Homebrella domains have been recycled by unrelated businesses; major home-warranty vendors returned blocks). Claims in this document are calibrated accordingly: capabilities observed across multiple sampled products are described as common; capabilities observed in a single product are described as product-specific or optional; the dedicated maintenance-plan pole is under-evidenced and is not characterized.\n257: \n258: Detailed evidence, product-by-product observations, cross-product comparison, and the historical/market-sample check are recorded in the paired Research Notes.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-auxiliary-pilot-2026-10-21/home-care/calls/discovery/leaf.md, lines 1-258\n1: # Home Maintenance Application\n2: \n3: ## Overview\n4: \n5: A **Home Maintenance Application** helps a homeowner stay on top of the recurring upkeep of their own home: it holds the home's care targets, turns them into a schedule of recurring care work, and keeps a record of the care that was done.\n6: \n7: The defining structure is small:\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: The user's own home (structure, systems, equipment) as the subject of care\n11: └── Recurring upkeep plan (care items with cadences → due work over time)\n12:     └── Upkeep record (completions accumulating into the home's care history)\n13: ```\n14: \n15: Everything else commonly associated with these products — reminder notifications, built-in task libraries, appliance databases with manuals and warranties, receipts, pro recommendations, rewards — is widespread in current products but is not part of the defining core. The paper home-maintenance schedule pinned in a house book satisfies the same structure without any of them.\n16: \n17: When the center of gravity shifts — to the household's routine labor (chores), to the whole-home information binder, to possession records, or to planned change-work — the product is drift